    e\left(\frac{ahf\overline{bs}}{c}\right)
    \right\vert^2,
\]
similar to \cite[p.\,23, third display]{maynard2025primes2}.
Here we also inserted a smooth majorant in the $f$ variable, where $\Psi_0$ is a compactly-supported nonnegative function satisfying $\Psi_0^{(j)} \ll_j 1$. As in \cite[p.\,23, second display]{maynard2025primes2}, the ranges $B, C_0, F_0$ satisfy
\begin{equation} \label{eq:bcf-bounds}
    B \ll NR, \qquad C_0 \ll NRS, \qquad F_0 \ll \frac{N}{Q},
\end{equation}
and as in \cite[(7.4)]{maynard2025primes2}, we need to show that $\mathscr{W}_4 \ll_{\varepsilon,a} x^{-6\varepsilon} N^2 R^2 S^3$. Normally at this stage, we would expand the square in $\mathscr{W}_4$, leading to a sum like in \cref{eq:incomplete-kloosterman-overview}, and then complete Kloosterman sums. But as outlined in \cref{subsec:improvements}, to achieve good savings in the complementary divisor ($f \sim F$) aspect, we will need to `move' $f$ to the other entry of the resulting Kloosterman sums. Towards this goal, we split the sum according to the value of $d = (f, c)$:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:W4-split}
