 \label{prop:fact-anatomy}
Let $\delta, \vartheta, u, v, N$ be as in \cref{lem:general-factorization}, $1 \le D \le x^{\vartheta-2\delta}$, and $d \in \mathcal{D}^{\textnormal{well}}(D)$. Write $d = p_1\cdots p_r$ where $p_1 \ge \cdots \ge p_r$ are primes. Assume that $p_1 \le x^u$, and that one of the following holds (statements involving $p_j$ implicitly assume $r \ge j$):
\begin{itemize}
    \item[$(i).$] $d_2 \in [x^v, x^u]$ for some $d_2 \in \{p_1, p_2, p_1p_2, p_1p_2p_3\}$;
    \item[$(ii).$] $d_2 := p_1p_3 \in [x^v, x^u]$ and $p_2^2 \le x^{\vartheta-2\delta}/d_2$;
    \item[$(iii).$] $d_2 := p_2p_3 \in [x^v, x^u]$ and $p_1^2 \le x^{\vartheta-2\delta}/d_2$;
    \item[$(iv).$] $p_3 \le x^{u-v}$, and ($p_1 \le x^v$, $p_2^2 \le x^{1-\vartheta-2\delta}$) or ($p_2 \le x^v$, $p_1^2 \le x^{1-\vartheta-2\delta}$).
\end{itemize}
Then there exists a factorization $d = d_1d_2d_3$ into positive integers satisfying \cref{eq:system-to-verify}.

Let $d = p_1\cdots p_r$ where $p_1 \ge \cdots \ge p_r$ are primes with $p_i = x^{t_i}$. Consider the set $I$ of all exponents $\vartheta_0 \in [0, 1)$ such that if $d \in \mathcal{D}^{\textnormal{well}}(x^{\vartheta_0})$, then there exists a factorization $d = d_1 d_2 d_3$ into positive integers obeying \cref{eq:system-to-verify}. Since $\mathcal{D}^{\textnormal{well}}(D) \subset \mathcal{D}^{\textnormal{well}}(D')$ when $D \le D'$, $I$ is an interval of the form $[0, \vartheta_{\max})$; our goal is to show that $\vartheta(t_1) - 2\delta \in I$ as in \cref{eq:vartheta-t}, and moreover, if $t_1 \le \tfrac{1}{4}$ and $r \ge 3$, then $\vartheta(t_1, t_2, t_3) - 4\delta \in I$ as in \cref{eq:vartheta-123}.

Applying \cref{prop:fact-anatomy} for some $\vartheta \in [\tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{5}{8}]$, we deduce that $\vartheta - 2\delta \in I$ provided $t_1 \le u$, and that one of the following holds:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[$(i)$.] $t \in [v, u]$ for some $t \in \{t_1, t_2, t_1 + t_2, t_1 + t_2 + t_3\}$;
    \item[$(ii)$.] $t_1 + t_3 \in [v, u]$ and $t_1 + 2t_2 + t_3 \le \vartheta - 2\delta$;
    \item[$(iii)$.] $t_2 + t_3 \in [v, u]$ and $2t_1 + t_2 + t_3 \le \vartheta - 2\delta$;
    \item[$(iv)$.] $t_3 \le u - v$ and $t_1 \le v$, $2t_2 \le 1 - \vartheta - 2\delta$;
    \item[$(v)$.] $t_3 \le u - v$ and $t_2 \le v$, $2t_1 \le 1 - \vartheta - 2\delta$.
\end{itemize}
Recalling the definitions of $\vartheta(t)$ and $u, v$ from \cref{eq:u-v-choice,eq:vartheta-t}, a short computation gives
\[
    t \in [u, v] = \left[2\vartheta - 1, \frac{2-2\vartheta}{3} \right] \qquad \iff \qquad \vartheta \le \vartheta(t) = \min\left(\frac{1+t}{2}, \frac{2-3t}{2}\right).
\]
Using $t = t_1$ in $(i)$, which automatically satisfies $t_1 \le u$, we deduce that 
\[
    \vartheta(t_1) - 2\delta \in I,
\]
as required. Now assume that $t_1 \le \tfrac{1}{4}$ and $r \ge 3$; then we have $t_1 \le \tfrac{1}{4} \le (2-2\vartheta)/3 = u$ for any $\vartheta \le \tfrac{5}{8}$, so the condition $t_1 \le u$ holds automatically. Thus points $(i)$-$(v)$ above imply, respectively:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[$(i)$.] $\max\{\vartheta(t_1), \vartheta(t_2), \vartheta(t_1+t_2), \vartheta(t_1+t_2+t_3)\} - 2\delta \in I$;
    \item[$(ii)$.] $\vartheta(t_1+t_3) - 2\delta \in I$ if the range $[t_1 + 2t_2 + t_3 + 2\delta, \vartheta(t_1+t_3)]$ is nonempty;
    \item[$(iii)$.] $\vartheta(t_2 + t_3) - 2\delta \in I$ if the range $[2t_1 + t_2 + t_3 + 2\delta, \vartheta(t_2+t_3)]$ is nonempty;
    \item[$(iv)$.] $\min\left\{ \tfrac{5-3t_3}{8}, 1-2t_2-2\delta\right \} - 2\delta \in I$ if $[\tfrac{1+t_1}{2}, \min\left\{ \tfrac{5-3t_3}{8}, 1-2t_2-2\delta\right \} - 2\delta]$ is nonempty;
    \item[$(v)$.] $\min\left\{ \tfrac{5-3t_3}{8}, 1-2t_1-2\delta\right \} - 2\delta \in I$ if $[\tfrac{1+t_2}{2}, \min\left\{ \tfrac{5-3t_3}{8}, 1-2t_1-2\delta\right \} - 2\delta]$ is nonempty.
\end{itemize}
Putting these together, using the cutoff function $\psi(x, y) = x \mathbbm{1}_{x \ge y+4\delta}$ to encode the nonemptiness of the required ranges above, and recalling \cref{eq:vartheta-123}, we conclude that
\[
    \vartheta(t_1, t_2, t_3) - 4\delta \in I,
\]
as required.

